#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134578} :activity/id #uuid "061434b6-5079-5b3b-b4da-2e73f02d7ea8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135186 :tutorial/content "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable. \n\nGiven a continuous random variable $X,$ the CDF is the function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nIf our random variable has the probability density function (or PDF) $f(x),$ then we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t) \\, \\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nNote that we use $f(t)dt$ in the integrand because $x$ serves as the upper limit of integration. \n\nFor example, consider the random variable $X$ with the following PDF:\n\n![](images/e7/e79c230f2b548c9280425f555f6d99a8f997e989289a05fb5f4584b656c4dcc8.svg)\n\nSince $f(x)= 0$ for all $x < 0,$  we have\n\n$$\nF(x)  = \\int_{-\\infty}^x 0 \\, \\textrm dt = 0\n$$\n\nif $x<0.$\n\nTo compute the CDF for $x\\geq 0,$  we integrate $f(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{2}{\\pi (1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing our CDF as a function defined over the entire real line, we get\n\n![](images/66/66b7bf54271075ae72fcf07fa4dc8573a94e97c2b2cc77680c0ec94b6053f360.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "de1bbdf6-687d-537a-a6fe-03d9fafbe841" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3f773be7-dfa4-524f-aec7-8ade124c5cea" :step/math-academy-id 132590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133516 :question/id #uuid "38b4473a-f956-5af5-863b-387dc10b4924" :question/math-academy-id "e-13362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d7509fa-a254-5b78-b307-3e0c4ef4d9ec" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With a Lower Bound"} :step/id #uuid "2411aa47-0b30-5e9c-b541-4d44b5f7aeed" :step/math-academy-id 132591 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133727 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133517 :question/id #uuid "98dd834c-ac74-5cdd-88ee-060fd9ac689d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b0ce833-e045-5aff-94a4-f9ad56cdb904" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442323}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a CDF Given a PDF That’s Nonzero Over All Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f242a3a5-626f-54b6-b0cc-b8af4506a3f0" :step/math-academy-id 132594 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135187 :tutorial/content "When a random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive on the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ only, we have\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 0$ for $x < a,$ and\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 1$ for $x > b.$\n\nIn these cases, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/7a/7a64bac7c252a3f52fc92f6364edb5c66f31e47da28e78eeb0d069d894135700.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0794419f-6b72-568b-a787-d71121c8bcd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8365 :tutorial/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "a74ce991-870f-5bfa-8a96-8bbed06ce5b6" :step/math-academy-id 132597 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133728 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133518 :question/id #uuid "4159a407-ea9f-5728-b219-bf1afab732a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eedcfa3d-b02b-5f96-99f4-cd0a452d272a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442325}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "47bc1334-f06b-5cc0-b191-427d1d862db4" :step/math-academy-id 132598 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135188 :tutorial/content "As with discrete random variables, we can use the CDF of a continuous random variable to quickly compute a probability over an interval:\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a random variable $X$ has the following CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}, \\qquad -\\infty < x < \\infty.\n$$\n\nWe can use the above CDF to quickly compute $P(0 \\leq x \\leq 1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le x\\le 1) & =F(1)-F(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.231\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval, so we have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 1) = P(0 < X \\leq 1) = P(0 \\leq X < 1) = P(0 < X < 1) = 0.231.\n$$\n\nIn general, for a continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = P(a < X < b) = F(b) - F(a).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5203710-822b-55d4-9dfb-985bc4d41d6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8366 :tutorial/title "Using a CDF to Compute a Probability Over an Interval"} :step/id #uuid "55418ca8-74f9-5755-9f75-2d411c4a476e" :step/math-academy-id 132601 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133729 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133519 :question/id #uuid "707e3b49-9c13-50d8-8a7f-c4d5ec44e0ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-13365"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbc399b-90f3-5acd-ac70-7345a8436978" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442322}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using a CDF"} :step/id #uuid "681dc276-df96-5b4d-b26c-338d9da62b88" :step/math-academy-id 132602}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134025}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424891} :activity/id #uuid "dc977e81-fd96-5729-9752-b43ae05cc6d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492f777b-8264-55ab-9be1-d1359bd9513d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424871} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a5540-6762-5260-9b7f-2a98061bf95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424872} {:answer/id #uuid "50e4b7f0-64ef-587b-bfde-a4520b836560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424873} {:answer/id #uuid "d60102cb-368c-5209-b3c3-06d9c44b89a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424880} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55c585-a616-50c9-9382-ffb7b5a8ceda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424881} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce55fccd-c2b9-544b-881d-b59a7e7c0f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b70eaac-0c45-5da5-8a2f-2002d9071035" :question/math-academy-id "q-89727" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/3|}}{6},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t/3}}{6}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x/3}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/3}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f2429d9-8a43-540c-8ee1-21b8c07473a4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424890}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f33f1e-6e04-5ef0-8a18-a9a9635e495a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424869} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc40166-55c4-53b3-bf06-562cddc8e868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424875} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0df617-e565-5181-bd27-09321bb7abfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424877} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e9b386-cf97-5734-82c8-f8b9318ea865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424878} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbc1dbf-3cbe-5835-99b1-f9050b0ed224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}-\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424875} :answer-field/id #uuid "5340e926-2580-5f2f-bf08-46d25e342346" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "97c91681-ff70-5452-bbfe-e1a92bb2e9d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126156" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}(x-1)(x-4),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}(t-1)(t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}-\\frac{2}{9}({t}^{2}-5t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2}{27}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{t}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x-(-\\frac{2}{27}+\\frac{5}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{27}{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{8}{9}x+\\frac{11}{27}, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9bf4404a-eca3-5f58-9aa8-59a46fdb29aa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424887 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d43dadc-2415-595b-83bd-07293e62a4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424868} {:answer/id #uuid "2d728c61-ee7a-5242-b976-dff68318b24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424870} {:answer/id #uuid "54302da3-4ffd-5ae6-9474-e5f95bb705f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424874} {:answer/id #uuid "86635ef4-b567-5842-a8be-7564b4bc47cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424876} {:answer/id #uuid "c53339f9-decf-5456-b59e-339275bad539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424870} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2e7998-6dc3-5838-ae14-0d427c2b4449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d72a8e6-eda0-59be-829b-e0298d5412e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-89695" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\le 5).$" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nHence, the required probability is $F(5)=1-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{4}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b81f8974-1a7b-53cb-bb5f-1b47021e293e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424889}}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424867}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442329} :activity/id #uuid "6e4f14fd-ece2-56c4-a9f7-319d868e1b1f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050750 :topic/id #uuid "6390206c-1add-5ea0-84f9-8288f56f20b5" :topic/math-academy-id 2163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a555ce7-328a-5393-a56d-7007de7c9835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442306} {:answer/id #uuid "2cdbdc75-2141-5c85-914f-cbf2e3062225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442307} {:answer/id #uuid "4760a79e-f036-5d0a-936b-74020feb11e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442308} {:answer/id #uuid "921eed10-f9a7-592f-813f-d6d89b83f924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442312} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9281eb-aea1-5259-9259-3205cf4277fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442315} :answer-field/id #uuid "20b56563-d267-5cfe-8c9b-169cce28e14c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "365bf094-f659-5822-bf63-2e5a6992d995" :question/math-academy-id "q-89745" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nHence, the required probability is $F(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{6}}=\\frac{63}{64}.$"} :step/id #uuid "24fe79a9-1c18-5367-b5d5-50f26230d23d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c66a62f-9a33-540e-bb1b-485e50f03db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442303} {:answer/id #uuid "12f66496-4995-5d37-a001-943fa360d144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442305} {:answer/id #uuid "5de59784-191d-59f0-ab4e-cb2aafa6fe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442309} {:answer/id #uuid "7295486c-b630-5ce0-8356-5ab47719adad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442311} {:answer/id #uuid "af97dd8a-6b84-534d-9a5b-b7c3f09bac24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442309} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e871fb-e944-567c-b4fe-9835a0a9327f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e65ef0d7-6649-5f56-9e79-7407f7595f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-89621" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{64}x(16-{x}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability density function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0<x<4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{64}t(16-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{64}(8{t}^{2}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4})|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}(8{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{256}, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "443e51e5-e403-51ae-bbff-e563149f49e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442326}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da4529e7-604e-506b-adaa-ff85312c20f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-x^{2}+8x-7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442316}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442316} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ff772f-b783-5f66-89ac-69df3d1d8f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "990f1bb6-e595-59df-8c02-d323344bc3ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-210785" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9}x+\\frac{8}{9},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $1\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}(-\\frac{2}{9}t+\\frac{8}{9})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={(-\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}t)|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}x-\\frac{7}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}({x}^{2}-8x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)(x-7), & 1\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a43148a-546c-5e25-817d-f6a542bfe3c4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442329 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96b44b4-1f7e-576d-9e77-9ec278a83d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133273} {:answer/id #uuid "107b6d59-d74b-5f3c-9bc5-01a9d06b20b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133276} {:answer/id #uuid "105177ed-381e-5551-8d54-f794cc7ae2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442304} {:answer/id #uuid "c6834059-6614-531c-86f9-081bd2ed69b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442314} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ff0cb6-5ee6-5486-95b5-ba7fabd16b87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442304} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3fece95-8a02-51e6-a7b8-03ddf8d52dc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d416c744-adfb-5c13-80ec-0e124bb7f438" :question/math-academy-id "q-89139" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ 1-{e}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X>2).$" :question/worked-solution "We can perform this computation using the complement, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-F(2) \\\\  & =1-(1-{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "989b9a58-4363-5017-8054-1b3f417bd0d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65441b2f-7b3a-53c5-af33-45bf0ced0a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442310}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442310} :answer-field/id #uuid "60abe397-ada8-52f7-89c3-1ccbe2f29023" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e1280de-34a4-5310-b0ea-3ca5ec3ba975" :question/math-academy-id "q-210763" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-|5x|},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-5t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cdf24c85-fec2-557e-990a-b96c38922ff0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442328}}] :activity/title "Cumulative Distribution Functions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442302}]}
